To create a highly accurate short-term forecast using your own Personal Weather Station (PWS) and a network of neighboring stations, you need to practice mesoscale analysis. By analyzing data across multiple points, you transform your single-point weather station into a live, 3D weather grid that can track storms, cold fronts, and temperature shifts as they move across your town.
Here is the professional framework for combining multiple local stations to predict your short-term weather.

1. Identify Your "Upwind Sentinel" Stations

Weather patterns in mid-latitudes generally move from west to east or southwest to northeast.
• Look at a crowd-sourced PWS map (like the Weather Underground Wundermap, Ambient Weather Network, or PWSweather).
• Locate 3 to 5 active stations situated 10 to 30 miles west and southwest of your home.
• Treat these stations as your "sentinels." Whatever weather, temperature drop, or wind spike happens at those stations will likely hit your house a short time later.

2. Calculate the "Speed of Onset"

By monitoring the exact time changes occur at upwind stations compared to their distance from you, you can calculate the exact arrival time of a weather system.
• The Formula: \(\text{Time to Arrival} = \frac{\text{Distance to Upwind Station}}{\text{Wind Speed or Frontal Speed}}\)
• Example: If a sentinel station 20 miles west of you experiences a sudden 10°F temperature drop and a shift to heavy rain, and the regional wind/storm line is moving east at 20 mph, that exact cold front will arrive at your front door in exactly 1 hour.

3. Build a Local Pressure Gradient (The Wind Predictor)

Wind is caused by air rushing from high pressure to low pressure. By comparing your barometer to neighboring barometers, you can predict local wind speed and direction changes hours in advance.
• Check the Delta: Look at stations 20 miles north, south, east, and west of you. Ensure their barometers are all calibrated to Sea Level Pressure.
• Predicting Wind Direction: If a station 15 miles north of you reads 1008 mb and your station reads 1012 mb, air will begin rushing toward the lower pressure. Expect your local winds to shift out of the South/Southwest to blow toward the North.
• Predicting Wind Speed: The greater the pressure difference (gradient) between your station and a neighbor, the stronger your local wind gusts will become over the next 2 to 3 hours.

4. Track Microclimate Boundaries (The Temperature Break)

If you live near mountains, valleys, or large bodies of water, regional weather apps completely miss local microclimates. Combining local PWS data lets you map these hidden boundaries:
• Sea/Lake Breezes: If you live within 15 miles of a large lake or ocean, watch the stations closest to the water. The moment their wind vanes flip to an onshore direction and their temperatures plummet, you can map the "sea breeze front." By watching how fast it moves through the inland PWS network, you can predict the exact minute your backyard will cool down.
• Cold Air Damming/Valley Inversions: In hilly or mountainous areas, check the stations located in the valleys versus those on the ridges. If valley stations show freezing temperatures while ridge stations are warm, you have an inversion. You can predict that freezing rain or dense valley fog will remain trapped at your house until the winds flip.

5. Monitor Live Humidity Convergence (Where Rain Fires Up)

Before rain or thunderstorms start, the air must saturate. You can predict exactly where a storm will pop up by looking at Dew Point trends across your local network.
• Look for an area on the local PWS map where dew points are rapidly rising and converging with the ambient temperature.
• If a cluster of stations 5 miles away shows the temperature and dew point getting within 2°F of each other while your station is still dry, air is violently rising in that zone. Rain clouds or thunderstorms are actively forming there and will move in your direction if the upper-level steering winds are blowing toward you.
